🎗️ 29.32 Why notifyAll() Instead of notify()?

Even though this implementation has only two threads, notifyAll() is a good choice because it clearly expresses:

The shared state has changed; all waiting threads should re-check their conditions.

Each thread has its own condition:

Zero Thread
→ !zeroFlag


Even-Odd Thread
→ zeroFlag == false

Only the thread whose condition is satisfied proceeds.

🎗️ 29.33 Why join() Is Not Used Here?

Your current Main starts both threads:

zeroThread.start();
evenOddThread.start();

but does not call:

zeroThread.join();
evenOddThread.join();

For this specific console program, the JVM will normally remain alive while these non-daemon worker threads are running.

However, if you want the main thread to explicitly wait for both threads, you can add:

zeroThread.join();
evenOddThread.join();

This is optional for the synchronization logic itself.

🎗️ 29.34 Common Mistakes
❌ Using separate SharedResource objects
❌ Using if instead of while before wait()
❌ Forgetting notifyAll()
❌ Forgetting to toggle zeroFlag
❌ Incrementing currNumber in Zero Thread
❌ Printing the number before Zero
❌ Not using synchronized methods
❌ Using busy waiting

🎗️ 29.35 Best Practices
✔ Use one shared SharedResource object
✔ Keep shared state inside SharedResource
✔ Use one monitor
✔ Use while before wait()
✔ Change shared state before notifyAll()
✔ Zero Thread only prints 0
✔ Even-Odd Thread owns currNumber
✔ Use boolean flag for this simple two-thread pattern

